  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/7/7b/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_120.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A network reporter named Allan Stevens is about to spend an entire night in a small cottage \"on the outskirts of the city\" to prove that there is no such thing as a haunted house.&nbsp; The entire thing is being covered by TV cameras, and Dr. Strange happens to walk by.&nbsp; He decides to stick around and observe.&nbsp; As the man enters the house, Strange decides to observe by sending his \"unphysical ectoplasmic form,\" but even that ghostly form cannot penetrate the house.&nbsp; Something is quite amiss here!\n\nFailing that, he peeks into the house with his all-seeing eye.&nbsp; Inside, the reporter is describing everything he sees for the broadcast audience when he suddenly says \"No!&nbsp; It can't be!&nbsp; It isn't possible.&nbsp; I must be going <i>mad</i>!!\"&nbsp; The TV audience isn't buying it, thinking it's all a trick, but Strange has seen enough and shoves everyone aside and enters the building himself!\n\nUpon entry, Dr. Strange confronts eerie mists and summons enchantments with very-impressive sounding words, finally pointing out that his power is greater.&nbsp; Soon Allan Stevens stumbles out, claiming that his mind went blank and doesn't know exactly what happened, but he swears he'll never go back!\n\nOnly then do we learn the secret of the house -- that it's actually <i>alive</i>, from \"another space-time continuum to observe us\" -- complete with a face that Strange talks to.. It's only <i>pretending</i> to be haunted so that \"none learn the truth!\"&nbsp; Strange agrees to keep this information to himself in return for Stevens being released.\n\nStrange leaves the house and then banishes it back to \"the shadow world from whence you came!\"&nbsp; Again, with the impressive words \"Let the vapors of the Vishanti drive you from the sight of man!&nbsp; Let the mystic hosts of Hoggoth prevent you from ever returning again!\"\n\nThe people observing think it's all a trick and that the house vanished via special effects.&nbsp; Even so, they clear the way for Dr. Strange as he walks away.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A haunted house turns out to be something far more eerie than expected.\nThen Dr. Strange arrives and makes the whole thing go away.",
  "subtitle": "Eviction Notice from the Vishanti",
  "review_text": "This was a story ready made for Dr. Strange to <i>happen</i> to wander by.&nbsp; He was able to rescue Allan Stevens, but the whole thing is staged in a manner that makes it come across as entirely accidental.\n\n[HANG0] A skeptical network reporter enters a haunted house while Dr. Strange watches from the street...\n\n[ASSET: left | MustBeGoingMad | width=50%] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=What could go <i>wrong</i>?]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ViewersNotBuyingIt | width=18%] [ASSET: right | StrangeIsGoingIn | width=26%] To the story's credit, Stan and Steve were determined to make sure that readers didn't forget about the fact that Allan Stevens' trip into the house was a television stunt.\n[BR]\n[BR] The co-creators even cut back as late as page 5 (of 9) to show a family watching the broadcast from inside their homes, but you could also tell that [SPAN: severity=warning; text=none of them were buying it!]\n[BR]\n[BR] Only Dr. Strange seemed to understand the danger, and he eventually shoved everyone aside and rushed to the rescue.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ImpressiveWords | width=12%] To no one's surprise, Strange almost immediately realizes the man is in trouble, which gives Stan the chance to make up very impressive dialogue to go along with Steve's mist-filled visuals on page 7.\n[BR]\n[BR] It takes Strange only 5 panels to overwhelm the house and demand the return of Stevens.\n\nStevens stumbles out, the house is banished, the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved], and Strange walks away in full view of the onlookers.\n[ASSET: center | WipingOfHands | width=large]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Good thing he happened to be walking by!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIf you really sit down and contemplate this story, it seems as staged as the opening television broadcast.\n\nThe \"old cottage\" that one observer noted she'd heard about \"for years\" is the very \"haunted house\" that Allan Stevens enters, all to prove to everyone that \"there's no such thing as a haunted house!\"\n\nThat's all well and good, but doesn't the fact that Dr. Strange just <i>happens by</i> seem [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=<i>way</i> beyond convenient?]\n\nEven if readers are willing to chalk that up to another case of [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Comic Book Convenience 101], in the final analysis, the events boil down to nothing more than the following:\n[HANG] * Skeptical reporter enters a haunted house in front of a televised audience\n[HANG] * Dr. Strange just <i>happens</i> to take a stroll that evening and observes the event\n[HANG] * Reporter learns the house really <i>is </i>haunted\n[HANG] * Strange forces his way in and learns the truth, rescuing the reporter\n[HANG] * The house is alive, sent from another continuum to \"observe\"\n[HANG] * Strange can't have that, so he overpowers the house and banishes it back\n[HANG] * Onlookers wonder what happened while Strange leaves\n\n[HANG0] And that may be the biggest problem of all.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Once the house is banished, <b>what exactly has changed?</b>]\n\nAllan Stevens has had a frightening experience.\n[BR] Dr. Strange has demonstrated that his power is greater than the house's.\n[BR] The reader has learned that the \"haunted house\" was actually alive.\n\nBeyond that, the story leaves almost no lasting impression.&nbsp; The living house arrives, briefly observes humanity for reasons never fully explained, and is promptly sent away again.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=The adventure ends almost exactly where it began.]\n\nThe story doesn't seem particularly interested in answering even its own mysteries:\n[HANG] * Why take the shape of a house?\n[HANG] * What was the \"house\" observing?\n[HANG] * Why has it taken \"years?\"\n\nThe house is simply sent away before any of those questions matter.\n\nAnd it also bears stating out loud:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=The living house sure didn't put up much of a fight!]\n\n[HANG0] Strange basically says \"My power is greater than yours!\"\n[BR] to which I say:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=That's your <b>story</b>?]\n\nSo much real estate is spent building the house into an overwhelming mystery, only for Strange to brush it aside in a matter of panels once the confrontation finally begins.\n\nI'm sorry, but this was just one more instance of a so-called story that essentially padded the page count with a very contrived scenario.&nbsp; It was one involving a Master of the Mystic Arts that writer Stan Lee makes sound very impressive through a collection of provocative incantations... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=and all for <i>what</i>?]\n\nApparently to banish a creepy \"old cottage\" back to its own space-time continuum.\n\nIs that worth nine story pages?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=I emphatically assert that the answer is <b>NO</b>!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Sigh!]\n\nDon't get me wrong:&nbsp; I'm happy that Dr. Strange was  able to rescue Allan Stevens, but I walk away wondering about the many others who undoubtedly had visited the cottage over the years who must have helped establish the rumor that it was haunted.&nbsp; We're never told if any of <i>them</i> made it out alive.&nbsp; And if they didn't, then the living house wasn't really just \"observing\", but rather... <i>um...</i> [SPAN: severity=error; text=gobbling them up] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(for lack of a better term)].\n\nSadly, the story relies on atmosphere, mystery, and impressive-sounding incantations to distract from how little actually happens.&nbsp; Remove the eerie cottage and mystical dialogue, and what's left is a story that begins with a problem and then simply has Dr. Strange make it go away.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=I'm going to need more than that!]\n\nThese Dr. Strange stories are all one-off adventures, which thus far seem set in their own little world, completely disconnected from the rest of the things happening in the Marvel Universe.\n\nEven the first panel of this month's story <i>mentions</i> that he doesn't get out of the house much, and yet everyone still seems to recognize him.&nbsp; And the final panel states that \"one thing is certain!&nbsp; There are <i>none</i> who will ever...<b><i>forget!!</i></b>\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=<i>Oh</i>, if I could only be so lucky!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nHaving read several letters from the readers of the day, it's clear that Doctor Strange seems quite popular with the audience, but for the life of me, I confess [SPAN: severity=error; text=I cannot spot the reason why.]\n\nIs it possible that there have just not been any comics addressing mystical things like this?&nbsp; Even <i>that</i> seems hard to comprehend, particularly with a title such as [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Strange Tales] (or even DC's [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=House of Mystery]).\n\nPerhaps this is the first time a single person has been the <i>focus</i> of such tales?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(That would make Dr. Strange out to be some kind of mystical Rod Serling -- someone to \"host\" the adventures.)]\n\nI confess I don't know the answers.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=All I can say is that I have remained unimpressed by Doctor Strange...]",
  "highlight": "A skeptical reporter, a living haunted house, and Dr. Strange all collide in a listless mystery that ends almost as soon as it begins."
}